Story: Your first hover
  As a new pilot with a VTOL or a quadcopter
  I want to lift off, hold a hover and bring the plane home to land
  So that I can fly the T1-S VTOL or the Seeker 3 from the sim alone, with no ground station

  This is the VTOL's [[Your first throw]]. The sim starts ArduPilot for itself, so start the
  sim with the T1-S VTOL (it is the default plane) or the Seeker 3 (`--aircraft seeker3`) and
  nothing else. Neither needs a throw. You arm it, and it takes off straight up in QHOVER,
  the first mode of the radio of both planes. In
  this tutorial you lift off, hold the hover, and then you send the plane home. It flies back
  to the pilot box and lands by itself.

  Scenario: Arm, lift off, hold the hover and bring the plane home
    Given the autopilot is disarmed
    When the pilot arms the plane
    Then the autopilot is armed
    When the pilot opens the throttle to climb
    Then the plane is flying
    When the pilot closes the throttle
    Then the plane is flying
    When the pilot sends the plane home
    Then the plane is on the ground

Story: Learning with self-levelling
  As a pilot learning to fly
  I want the plane to level itself when I let go of the sticks
  So that I can learn the controls without it getting away from me

  In [[ANGLE]], the plane levels its wings when you release the sticks. Thus the plane stays
  under control while you learn. In this tutorial, you select ANGLE, throw the plane and roll
  it. Then you release the stick, and the wings become level again. The VTOL has the same
  help in FBWA: see [[Flying a VTOL with self-levelling]].

  Scenario: Pick ANGLE mode and throw
    When the pilot changes flight mode
    Then the flight mode is ANGLE
    When the pilot throws the plane with the throttle
    Then the plane is flying
    When the pilot rolls the plane to the left
    Then the wings are banked
    When the pilot releases the roll stick
    Then the wings are level
